Basics of Shell Scripting Concepts:
1. Hello World example:
#!/bin/bash
echo "Hello, Linkedin!"
👉 "#!/bin/bash" is the shebang line that tells the system which interpreter to use.
👉 "echo" prints to the terminal.

2.Make Script Executable:
chmod +x script.sh
./script.sh
👉 "chmod"= change mode — used to change file permissions.
👉 "+x" = adds executable permission.
👉 script.sh = the file you are modifying.
👉 By default, a ".sh" file (shell script) is just a text file. This command tells the system: “Hey, this script is safe and should be treated like an executable program.”
👉 "./ "= tells the shell to look in the current directory.
🎯 Why use ./?
Your shell doesn’t usually search the current directory (.) by default for security reasons. So, you explicitly tell it: “Run the script.sh that’s in this folder.”
When you're not in the current directory, 
👉 bash /bash_scripts/deploy.sh     # Use bash with Full Path
👉 /bash_scripts/deploy.sh             # Use the Full (Absolute) Path, Only works if the script is executable (chmod +x is set).

3.Variables and Data Types:
name="Linkedin"
echo "Hello $name"
👉 "name" is the variable name.
👉 "Linkedin" is the string value assigned to it.
📌 NOTE: No spaces are allowed around = in Bash.
👉 "echo'" prints output to the terminal.
👉 "$name'" accesses the value stored in the name variable.
👉 The "$name" is replaced with "Linkedin" at runtime.

✅ Final Output:
Hello Linkedin

4.If-Else:
#!/bin/bash
if [ "$1" == "start" ]; then
  echo "Starting the service..."
else
  echo "Unknown command"
fi
👉 $1 refers to the first command-line argument passed to the script.
👉 if [ "$1" == "start" ]; then - This checks if the first argument is equal to the string start.
👉 == is used for string comparison.
👉 echo "Starting the service..."  This command will be executed only if $1 is "start".
👉 else: Executes if the condition in the if is not true.
👉 echo "Unknown command": This will run if the argument was something other than "start".
👉 fi:This marks the end of the if block in Bash.
Example1: ./script.sh start
✅ Output: Starting the service...
Example 2: ./script.sh stop
✅ Output: Unknown command

5.Functions:
greet() {
 echo "Hello $1"
}
greet "Engineer"
👉 greet(): This defines a function in Bash called greet. Functions let you group reusable code
👉 $1 refers to the first argument passed to the function.
📌 In shell scripting: • $0 is the script name. • $1, $2, etc., are positional parameters—they hold the arguments passed to a script or a function.
👉 greet "Engineer": You are calling the function greet and passing it the string "Engineer". Inside the function, $1 gets the value "Engineer"
🧾 What Happens When You Run This:
•Bash sees the function call: greet "Engineer"
•It calls the greet function, passing "Engineer" as the first argument.
•Inside the function, $1 becomes "Engineer"
•The function runs echo "Hello $1", which becomes: echo "Hello Engineer"
✅ Output:   Hello Engineer
